      "raw_text": "Here is the step-by-step solution to the problem:\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the distance the horse runs.**\nThe horse runs for 30 minutes at a speed of 32 miles per hour (MPH).\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour, 30 minutes is equal to $0.5$ hours.\n$$ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} $$\n$$ \\text{Distance} = 32 \\text{ mph} \\times 0.5 \\text{ hours} = 16 \\text{ miles} $$\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the total amount of hay needed.**\nThe problem states that for every mile the horse runs, it eats $1/2$ a bail of hay.\n$$ \\text{Total hay} = \\text{Distance} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ bail/mile} $$\n$$ \\text{Total hay} = 16 \\times 0.5 = 8 \\text{ bails of hay} $$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total cost of the hay.**\nOne bail of hay costs $\\$3$.\n$$ \\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Total hay} \\times \\text{Cost per bail} $$\n$$ \\text{Total Cost} = 8 \\times \\$3 = \\$24 $$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total money Michael has.**\nMichael has six $\\$5$ bills.\n$$ \\text{Total Money} = 6 \\times \\$5 = \\$30 $$\n\n**Step 5: Calculate the change.**\nSubtract the total cost of the hay from the total money Michael has.\n$$ \\text{Change} = \\text{Total Money} - \\text{Total Cost} $$\n$$ \\text{Change} = \\$30 - \\$24 = \\$6 $$\n\n#### 6<|im_end|>",
      "answer_text": "Here is the step-by-step solution to the problem:\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the distance the horse runs.**\nThe horse runs for 30 minutes at a speed of 32 miles per hour (MPH).\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour, 30 minutes is equal to $0.5$ hours.\n$$ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} $$\n$$ \\text{Distance} = 32 \\text{ mph} \\times 0.5 \\text{ hours} = 16 \\text{ miles} $$\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the total amount of hay needed.**\nThe problem states that for every mile the horse runs, it eats $1/2$ a bail of hay.\n$$ \\text{Total hay} = \\text{Distance} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ bail/mile} $$\n$$ \\text{Total hay} = 16 \\times 0.5 = 8 \\text{ bails of hay} $$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total cost of the hay.**\nOne bail of hay costs $\\$3$.\n$$ \\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Total hay} \\times \\text{Cost per bail} $$\n$$ \\text{Total Cost} = 8 \\times \\$3 = \\$24 $$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total money Michael has.**\nMichael has six $\\$5$ bills.\n$$ \\text{Total Money} = 6 \\times \\$5 = \\$30 $$\n\n**Step 5: Calculate the change.**\nSubtract the total cost of the hay from the total money Michael has.\n$$ \\text{Change} = \\text{Total Money} - \\text{Total Cost} $$\n$$ \\text{Change} = \\$30 - \\$24 = \\$6 $$\n\n#### 6",
